WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.338 position:50% size:92% align:center
The four-thread block writes values one through

00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:05.144 position:50% size:92% align:center
four into its shared workspace.

00:00:05.364 --> 00:00:08.235 position:50% size:92% align:center
A block barrier follows those writes. Only after

00:00:08.235 --> 00:00:09.790 position:50% size:92% align:center
it do the selected readers

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.465 position:50% size:92% align:center
consume neighbouring values.

00:00:13.485 --> 00:00:16.380 position:50% size:92% align:center
All four non-exited block threads must reach the

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.371 position:50% size:92% align:center
matching barrier in this example.

00:00:18.591 --> 00:00:21.324 position:50% size:92% align:center
Having enough SM resources does not repair a

00:00:21.324 --> 00:00:24.120 position:50% size:92% align:center
missing participant. The barrier contract and

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.103 position:50% size:92% align:center
the hardware’s capacity are different questions.

00:00:29.123 --> 00:00:31.963 position:50% size:92% align:center
With two readers, threads zero and one consume

00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:33.692 position:50% size:92% align:center
shared values after all four

00:00:33.692 --> 00:00:34.989 position:50% size:92% align:center
writers have arrived.

00:00:35.209 --> 00:00:37.804 position:50% size:92% align:center
The condition selects consumers. It does not

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.692 position:50% size:92% align:center
select which writers participate

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:41.225 position:50% size:92% align:center
in the earlier collective.

00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:45.896 position:50% size:92% align:center
Consider readers equal to one, with the same

00:00:45.896 --> 00:00:48.365 position:50% size:92% align:center
four launched threads and no early exits.

00:00:48.585 --> 00:00:51.683 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict the required number of barrier

00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:53.748 position:50% size:92% align:center
arrivals. Is it now one or four?

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:02.254 position:50% size:92% align:center
The required arrivals stay at four. Only the

00:01:02.254 --> 00:01:03.963 position:50% size:92% align:center
number of consumers changes.

00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:06.800 position:50% size:92% align:center
The safe program leaves the barrier outside the

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.917 position:50% size:92% align:center
reader condition, so the shared writes

00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:10.476 position:50% size:92% align:center
precede the dependent reads.

00:01:12.496 --> 00:01:15.224 position:50% size:92% align:center
If some of these non-exited threads skip a

00:01:15.224 --> 00:01:18.083 position:50% size:92% align:center
barrier taken by the others, this is invalid

00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:19.707 position:50% size:92% align:center
collective participation.

00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:22.880 position:50% size:92% align:center
The diagnostic does not assign a numerical

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.184 position:50% size:92% align:center
result or promise how a real GPU will fail. Fix

00:01:26.184 --> 00:01:28.012 position:50% size:92% align:center
the participant set before

00:01:28.012 --> 00:01:29.911 position:50% size:92% align:center
reasoning about the output.
